"""Class 6 Science Chapter 10 Data Module"""

CHAPTER_META = {'title': 'Living Creatures: Exploring their Characteristics', 'class_grade': 'Class 6', 'chapter_num': 10, 'theme': 'Curiosity • NEP 2020 Science', 'short_titles': ['Alive or Not', 'Move & Grow', 'Breathe Deep', 'Waste Out', 'Touch & Sense', 'Life Goes On', 'Sprout Needs', 'Seed Drink', 'Soil Breathers', 'Sun & Shadow', 'Root & Shoot', 'J.C. Bose', 'Plant Cycle', 'Mosquito Four', 'Silk Cocoon', 'Frog Jump', 'Challenge 1', 'Challenge 2', 'Challenge 3', 'Challenge 4']}

SLIDES = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Alive or Not', 'subtitle': 'Discovering Life in Surprising Forms & The Mystery of Snail Shells', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>During a crisp morning walk, two siblings, Avadhi and Aayush, noticed beautiful spiral shells lying on moist garden soil. Aayush assumed the shells were merely pretty stones. Their mother explained an astonishing truth: each shell was once part of the living body of a garden snail, and some shells might still house a sleeping snail inside!</p>\n            <p class='mt-2'>This observation raises a fundamental scientific question: <strong>What truly distinguishes a living creature from a non-living object?</strong> While a car moves and consumes fuel, it cannot grow, heal, or reproduce. A lifeless stone remains unchanged for centuries, but a living snail feeds, breathes, responds to touch, reproduces, and eventually dies.</p>\n            <div class='mt-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 text-xs text-indigo-300'>\n                🌱 <strong>Key Principle:</strong> Life cannot be defined by a single property like movement. It requires an integrated set of vital processes: nutrition, growth, respiration, excretion, response to stimuli, reproduction, and cellular organization.\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Welcome to our exploration of living creatures and their unique characteristics. During a morning walk, Avadhi and Aayush discovered smooth spiral shells on the damp soil. While an empty shell might look like an ordinary pebble, it was crafted by a living snail as its protective home. This brings us to a fundamental question in science: how do we distinguish between living beings and non-living objects? A wooden pencil or a carved stone remains unchanged, whereas living creatures eat, grow, breathe, eliminate waste, sense their environment, reproduce, and undergo a life cycle. Let us explore the remarkable hallmarks of life.', 'lecture_script': 'Welcome to our exploration of living creatures and their unique characteristics. During a morning walk, Avadhi and Aayush discovered smooth spiral shells on the damp soil. While an empty shell might look like an ordinary pebble, it was crafted by a living snail as its protective home. This brings us to a fundamental question in science: how do we distinguish between living beings and non-living objects? A wooden pencil or a carved stone remains unchanged, whereas living creatures eat, grow, breathe, eliminate waste, sense their environment, reproduce, and undergo a life cycle. Let us explore the remarkable hallmarks of life.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why is a car that moves along a highway classified as non-living, whereas a resting snail is classified as living?', 'options': ['A car runs on petrol while a snail does not move at all', 'A car cannot grow, respire, excrete, or reproduce on its own', 'A car has metal parts while snails have no hard body parts', 'A car requires human driving while animals do not consume fuel'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Movement alone does not define life. A car lacks autonomous cellular metabolism, cannot grow, cannot reproduce, and cannot heal or respond to stimuli biologically.', 'study_insight': 'Never judge life by motion alone; evaluate the full spectrum of vital physiological traits.'}, {'question': 'What role does the hard shell play in the biology of a living land snail?', 'options': ["It is an artificial rock glued to the snail's body", 'It is an exoskeleton that protects soft organs and prevents moisture loss', 'It generates food through photosynthesis when the snail sleeps', 'It serves as a swimming fin for aquatic locomotion'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "A snail's shell is an organic-calcium exoskeleton grown by its mantle tissue, shielding delicate organs from predators and dehydration.", 'study_insight': 'Even dormant or retracted animals possess specialized biological adaptations essential for survival.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Move & Grow', 'subtitle': 'Locomotion in Animals, Plant Tropisms & Irreversible Cellular Growth', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Living organisms exhibit distinct patterns of <strong>movement</strong> and <strong>growth</strong> fueled by nutrition:</p>\n            <ul class='list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                <li><strong>Locomotion in Animals:</strong> Animals move from place to place using limbs, fins, or muscular feet to search for food, find shelter, and escape predators.</li>\n                <li><strong>Movement in Plants:</strong> Rooted in soil, plants cannot walk, yet they exhibit active internal and directional movements. Flowers open to greet sunrise and close at night. Tendrils of climbing peas coil tightly around trellises.</li>\n                <li><strong>Carnivorous Plants:</strong> The insectivorous sundew (<em>Drosera</em>) possesses glandular leaf hairs tipped with sticky droplets. When an unwary insect lands, the tentacles bend inward within seconds to trap and digest it!</li>\n                <li><strong>Growth & Nutrition:</strong> All living beings take in nourishment to build new cells. Unlike a growing sand dune that merely accumulates surface dust, biological growth is internal, cellular, and irreversible.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Animals display rapid locomotion, running, flying, or swimming across their habitats in search of food and safety. Plants cannot change their location, yet they exhibit fascinating biological movements. Sunflowers turn to track the sun, climbing tendrils wrap around fences, and the carnivorous Drosera sundew snaps its sticky glandular hairs inward to trap unsuspecting insects. Furthermore, all living things undergo irreversible growth. A tiny seed expands into a towering banyan tree, and a human infant matures into an adult through continuous cellular division powered by wholesome nutrition.', 'lecture_script': 'Animals display rapid locomotion, running, flying, or swimming across their habitats in search of food and safety. Plants cannot change their location, yet they exhibit fascinating biological movements. Sunflowers turn to track the sun, climbing tendrils wrap around fences, and the carnivorous Drosera sundew snaps its sticky glandular hairs inward to trap unsuspecting insects. Furthermore, all living things undergo irreversible growth. A tiny seed expands into a towering banyan tree, and a human infant matures into an adult through continuous cellular division powered by wholesome nutrition.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How does the insectivorous plant Drosera (Sundew) demonstrate biological movement without possessing animal muscles?', 'options': ['It detaches its roots and chases insects across the bog', 'Its glandular leaf tentacles bend inward in response to the touch of prey', 'It blows air bubbles to knock flying insects onto the ground', 'It generates high-voltage electric shocks to paralyze nearby bugs'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Drosera uses thigmonastic leaf movement where sensory hairs trigger rapid differential cell turgor changes, curling tentacles inward to envelop prey.', 'study_insight': 'Plants demonstrate dynamic physiological movement even without bones or animal muscle fibers.'}, {'question': 'How does biological growth in a living puppy differ fundamentally from the enlargement of a growing desert sand dune?', 'options': ['A puppy grows by accumulating external sand on its skin', 'Biological growth occurs from within via cell division and metabolism', 'A sand dune consumes protein and milk to expand its volume', 'There is no scientific difference between biological and physical growth'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Non-living growth (like a sand dune or crystal) occurs by external accretion of particles, whereas living growth happens from within via cellular multiplication.', 'study_insight': 'True biological growth is irreversible and driven by internal metabolic synthesis of organic matter.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Breathe Deep', 'subtitle': 'Cellular Respiration, Gaseous Exchange & Microscopic Leaf Stomata', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Every living organism requires a continuous supply of energy to sustain life. This energy is unlocked through <strong>respiration</strong>:</p>\n            <ul class='list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                <li><strong>Breathing vs. Respiration:</strong> Breathing is the physical inhalation of oxygen-rich air and exhalation of carbon dioxide. Respiration is the cellular biochemical reaction where oxygen burns digested food to release energy (ATP).</li>\n                <li><strong>Animal Adaptations:</strong> Humans and mammals breathe through lungs, fish extract dissolved oxygen using feathered gills, earthworms breathe through moist skin, and insects use tracheal air tubes.</li>\n                <li><strong>Plant Stomata:</strong> Plants also respire day and night! Leaves contain thousands of microscopic pores called <strong>stomata</strong> (singular: stoma), flanked by kidney-shaped guard cells. Stomata exchange gases: absorbing oxygen for respiration and releasing carbon dioxide, as well as absorbing carbon dioxide for daytime photosynthesis.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'All living creatures must respire to unlock energy from their food. While breathing is the physical act of inhaling and exhaling, cellular respiration is the chemical combustion of nutrients to fuel daily activities. Humans and birds utilize lungs, fish harvest dissolved oxygen with delicate gills, and earthworms exchange gases directly across their moist skin. Plants are active breathers too. On the undersides of their green leaves lie thousands of microscopic pores called stomata. Regulated by specialized guard cells, these stomata take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide continuously throughout day and night.', 'lecture_script': 'All living creatures must respire to unlock energy from their food. While breathing is the physical act of inhaling and exhaling, cellular respiration is the chemical combustion of nutrients to fuel daily activities. Humans and birds utilize lungs, fish harvest dissolved oxygen with delicate gills, and earthworms exchange gases directly across their moist skin. Plants are active breathers too. On the undersides of their green leaves lie thousands of microscopic pores called stomata. Regulated by specialized guard cells, these stomata take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide continuously throughout day and night.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which microscopic structures on plant leaves perform gas exchange for respiration and photosynthesis?', 'options': ['Chlorophyll pigment sacs', 'Stomata guarded by specialized guard cells', 'Xylem vessels in the midrib', 'Pollen tubes on the petal margin'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Stomata are microscopic apertures distributed primarily on the lower epidermis of leaves, opening and closing to regulate gas exchange and transpiration.', 'study_insight': 'Plants continuously respire 24 hours a day, taking in oxygen and giving off carbon dioxide.'}, {'question': 'Why does an earthworm emerge onto the soil surface during heavy torrential monsoon rains?', 'options': ['It seeks direct sunlight to tan its skin', 'Waterlogged soil pores displace air, depriving its moist skin of oxygen', 'It needs to lay eggs in fast-flowing rainwater streams', 'It prefers swimming over crawling in subterranean burrows'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Earthworms breathe through moist skin by absorbing oxygen trapped in soil air spaces. When floodwaters saturate these air pockets, earthworms must surface to avoid suffocation.', 'study_insight': 'Underground life forms depend completely on the aeration of soil pores for respiration.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Waste Out', 'subtitle': 'Metabolic Excretion in Animals & Guttation Droplets in Flora', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Metabolic chemical reactions within living bodies produce toxic by-products that must be eliminated. The process of expelling metabolic waste is called <strong>excretion</strong>.</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3'>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase'>Animal Excretion</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>Animals filter blood using kidneys to form urine, venting urea and excess salts. The skin releases sweat to discharge water, mineral salts, and maintain thermal cooling. Lungs exhale carbon dioxide.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 uppercase'>Plant Excretion</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>Plants lack kidneys, yet they effectively remove waste! Toxic minerals are stored in old leaves or bark that shed periodically. Early in the morning, plants like garden grasses, strawberry leaves, and roses exude excess water and dissolved salts as tiny droplets through specialized leaf-margin hydathodes—a phenomenon called <strong>guttation</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'As chemical reactions hum inside living cells, waste products inevitably accumulate. If retained, these toxic by-products would poison the organism. Animals rely on complex excretory systems: kidneys filter blood to produce urine, sweat glands release salts and water through the skin, and lungs expel carbon dioxide. Plants excrete waste through clever botanical methods. They sequester unwanted minerals inside old leaves and bark that fall away with the seasons. Furthermore, on humid mornings, plants like garden grasses and roses push out excess water and mineral salts as sparkling droplets along leaf margins through a process known as guttation.', 'lecture_script': 'As chemical reactions hum inside living cells, waste products inevitably accumulate. If retained, these toxic by-products would poison the organism. Animals rely on complex excretory systems: kidneys filter blood to produce urine, sweat glands release salts and water through the skin, and lungs expel carbon dioxide. Plants excrete waste through clever botanical methods. They sequester unwanted minerals inside old leaves and bark that fall away with the seasons. Furthermore, on humid mornings, plants like garden grasses and roses push out excess water and mineral salts as sparkling droplets along leaf margins through a process known as guttation.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the phenomenon called when morning grass blades exude excess water and mineral droplets from leaf margins?', 'options': ['Photosynthetic precipitation', 'Guttation through specialized hydathodes', 'Condensation of ambient clouds', 'Sublimation of leaf cuticle'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Guttation is the exudation of xylem sap droplets from the tips or edges of leaves of certain vascular plants, facilitated by root pressure during high humidity.', 'study_insight': 'Guttation originates from internal plant vascular pressure, unlike dew which condenses from external atmosphere.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following is a recognized mechanism by which deciduous trees dispose of accumulated mineral wastes?', 'options': ['Shedding aged, yellowing leaves and peeling dead outer bark', 'Absorbing waste minerals back into newly developing fruit seeds', 'Pumping toxic substances into surrounding pollinating flowers', 'Converting all inorganic minerals into gaseous atmospheric nitrogen'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Plants store non-recyclable metabolic by-products in cellular vacuoles of mature leaves and bark. When these shed in autumn, the tree is cleansed of waste.', 'study_insight': 'Abscission (leaf shedding) serves both seasonal water preservation and excretory waste elimination.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Touch & Sense', 'subtitle': 'Irritability, Rapid Reflexes & The Sensitive Mimosa and Amla Leaves', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Any sudden change in an organism's environment that evokes a physiological or behavioral reaction is called a <strong>stimulus</strong> (plural: stimuli). The capacity of living beings to perceive and react to stimuli is known as <strong>irritability</strong> or sensitivity.</p>\n            <ul class='list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1.5 text-slate-300'>\n                <li><strong>Animal Responses:</strong> If your hand inadvertently touches a scorching kettle or a sharp cactus spine, your nervous system reflexively yanks your arm back within milliseconds before you even feel conscious pain!</li>\n                <li><strong>The Sensitive Touch-Me-Not (<em>Mimosa pudica</em>):</strong> Locally called <em>Chhui-mui</em> or <em>Lajwanti</em>, touching its feathery green leaflets triggers rapid loss of water pressure from swollen leaf bases (pulvini), causing the leaflets to fold shut in under a second!</li>\n                <li><strong>Sleeping Leaves of Amla:</strong> The Indian gooseberry (<em>Amla</em>) tree displays nyctinasty: its pinnate leaves remain wide open under daytime sun to capture light, but close and droop softly as dusk falls.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Living organisms are exquisitely attuned to their surroundings. A stimulus is any change in the external or internal environment that provokes an active response. When you accidentally touch a sharp thorn or a burning pan, your hand jerks back instantaneously in a protective reflex. Plants display equally striking responses. The famous touch-me-not plant, known as Chhui-mui or Mimosa pudica, folds its delicate leaflets and droops within seconds of the gentlest tap. Similarly, the leaves of the amla tree undergo sleeping movements, spreading wide in sunlight and folding tightly shut after twilight.', 'lecture_script': 'Living organisms are exquisitely attuned to their surroundings. A stimulus is any change in the external or internal environment that provokes an active response. When you accidentally touch a sharp thorn or a burning pan, your hand jerks back instantaneously in a protective reflex. Plants display equally striking responses. The famous touch-me-not plant, known as Chhui-mui or Mimosa pudica, folds its delicate leaflets and droops within seconds of the gentlest tap. Similarly, the leaves of the amla tree undergo sleeping movements, spreading wide in sunlight and folding tightly shut after twilight.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What botanical mechanism causes the leaflets of the Chhui-mui (Mimosa pudica) plant to collapse rapidly upon physical touch?', 'options': ['Rapid loss of water turgor pressure from the pulvinus cells at leaf bases', 'Snapping of muscular tendons within the leaf veins', 'Instantaneous chemical drying and death of the touched foliage', 'Release of toxic acidic sap that dissolves the leaf stalk'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Mechanical touch triggers an electrical-chemical signal causing potassium ions and water to rush out of the lower pulvinus cells, causing sudden loss of turgor and folding.', 'study_insight': 'Plant responsiveness (thigmonasty) demonstrates that plants perceive physical contact dynamically.'}, {'question': "How do the 'sleeping leaves' of the Amla tree respond to diurnal changes in ambient sunlight?", 'options': ['They fall off the branches every single evening and regrow at dawn', 'They remain open in daytime sunlight and close/droop after sunset', 'They emit glowing bioluminescent light during the darkest hours of night', 'They curl into spiky needles to prevent nocturnal bird perching'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Nyctinasty is a rhythmic response to light-dark cycles where amla leaflets open under daylight for photosynthesis and close at dusk to conserve heat and moisture.', 'study_insight': 'Living organisms possess endogenous biological rhythms tuned to daily environmental shifts.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Life Goes On', 'subtitle': 'Biological Reproduction, Continuity of Species & The Inevitability of Death', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Every individual organism has a finite lifespan, yet life on Earth has persisted unbroken for billions of years. This continuity is made possible by <strong>reproduction</strong>.</p>\n            <div class='space-y-2 mt-2'>\n                <p><strong>Reproduction:</strong> The biological process by which existing living organisms produce new individuals of their own kind:</p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>Plants:</strong> Flowering plants generate seeds within fruits, which sprout into new generations. Many plants can also reproduce through vegetative stems (potatoes, ginger) or leaves (Bryophyllum).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Animals:</strong> Some animals lay eggs (oviparous, such as birds, reptiles, and fish), while others give birth to live young (viviparous, such as cows, elephants, and humans).</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='mt-2'><strong>Death:</strong> Every living creature eventually completes its life span. Senescence leads to death—the complete and irreversible cessation of all metabolic processes, heartbeat, breathing, and cellular repair.</p>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Because every individual living being has a limited lifespan, the survival of any species depends upon reproduction. Reproduction is the biological process through which organisms generate offspring of their own kind. Birds and frogs lay eggs that hatch into offspring, mammals give birth to live young, and flowering plants produce seeds nestled inside nourishing fruits. When an organism reaches the end of its natural life, death occurs, marking the final cessation of breathing, circulation, and cellular energy production. Reproduction ensures that even as individuals perish, the grand tapestry of life continues uninterrupted.', 'lecture_script': 'Because every individual living being has a limited lifespan, the survival of any species depends upon reproduction. Reproduction is the biological process through which organisms generate offspring of their own kind. Birds and frogs lay eggs that hatch into offspring, mammals give birth to live young, and flowering plants produce seeds nestled inside nourishing fruits. When an organism reaches the end of its natural life, death occurs, marking the final cessation of breathing, circulation, and cellular energy production. Reproduction ensures that even as individuals perish, the grand tapestry of life continues uninterrupted.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What would inevitably happen to a species of birds if every member ceased to reproduce?', 'options': ['The birds would become immortal and live forever', 'The birds would evolve into reptiles within one generation', 'The species would gradually decline as individuals age and become extinct', 'The birds would reproduce by splitting their feathers into new chicks'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Because individuals die due to aging, disease, or predation, reproduction is the sole biological mechanism preventing species extinction.', 'study_insight': 'Reproduction maintains genetic continuity across generations across all kingdoms of life.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following pairs correctly matches an organism with its primary mode of reproduction?', 'options': ['Pigeon — gives birth to live puppies', 'Mango tree — produces seeds inside juicy drupe fruits', 'Cow — lays hard-shelled eggs in sandy nests', 'Frog — reproduces exclusively by shedding green leaves'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Mango trees produce seeds inside their fruits that germinate into new saplings; cows are viviparous mammals; pigeons and frogs lay eggs.', 'study_insight': 'Different biological groups employ diverse reproductive strategies tailored to their environmental niches.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Sprout Needs', 'subtitle': 'Activity 10.2: Scientific Investigation into Germination Parameters', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>A dry seed appears hard, motionless, and completely dormant. Is it dead or alive? Inside lies a living plant embryo awaiting the right environmental cues to awaken! In <strong>Activity 10.2</strong>, we investigate the exact environmental factors required for seed germination:</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2 mt-3 text-center'>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-lg'>💧</div>\n                    <div class='text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1'>Water</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Softens testa & hydrates enzymes</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-lg'>💨</div>\n                    <div class='text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1'>Air (Oxygen)</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Aerobic respiration for energy</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-lg'>🌡️</div>\n                    <div class='text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1'>Warmth</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Optimal 20°C to 30°C temperature</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-lg'>☀️</div>\n                    <div class='text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1'>Light/Dark</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Sprouts in dark, needs light after</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-3'>By setting up controlled batches of gram (chana) or moong seeds under varied conditions (completely dry, submerged in boiled water without air, chilled in ice, or in moist warm soil), we discover that <strong>water, oxygen, and moderate warmth</strong> are non-negotiable prerequisites!</p>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'A dry chickpea or moong seed seems completely dormant and lifeless. Yet, nestled securely within its tough coat is a living baby plant embryo. In Activity 10.2, we conduct a controlled investigation to determine what triggers this embryo to sprout. By testing seeds under different environmental conditions, we discover that three factors are absolutely vital: moisture to soften the seed coat, fresh air providing oxygen for respiration, and comfortable warmth to catalyze cellular enzymes. Without these, the embryo remains fast asleep in dormancy.', 'lecture_script': 'A dry chickpea or moong seed seems completely dormant and lifeless. Yet, nestled securely within its tough coat is a living baby plant embryo. In Activity 10.2, we conduct a controlled investigation to determine what triggers this embryo to sprout. By testing seeds under different environmental conditions, we discover that three factors are absolutely vital: moisture to soften the seed coat, fresh air providing oxygen for respiration, and comfortable warmth to catalyze cellular enzymes. Without these, the embryo remains fast asleep in dormancy.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which set of environmental conditions is absolutely essential for the initial germination of bean seeds?', 'options': ['Direct ultraviolet radiation, zero moisture, and freezing cold', 'Water, adequate oxygen from air, and suitable moderate warmth', 'Artificial fertilizers, complete vacuum, and boiling heat', 'Continuous darkness, zero oxygen, and high salinity'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Seed germination strictly requires moisture to hydrate tissues, oxygen for cellular respiration, and favorable warmth for enzymatic reactions.', 'study_insight': 'A controlled fair-test isolates one environmental variable at a time while keeping others constant.'}, {'question': 'What happens to dry gram seeds kept on a dry cotton pad at room temperature for several weeks?', 'options': ['They germinate immediately due to room temperature warmth', 'They remain dormant and fail to sprout because water is completely missing', 'They rot rapidly into liquid mud within three days', 'They spontaneously burst into green leaves without roots'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Without water to trigger imbibition and activate metabolic enzymes, dry seeds remain in a state of suspended animation known as seed dormancy.', 'study_insight': 'Dormancy protects seeds from sprouting in dry, unviable conditions.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Seed Drink', 'subtitle': 'Water Imbibition, Testa Softening & Metabolic Enzyme Activation', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Why is water the spark of germination? When dry seeds are soaked in water overnight, dramatic biophysical transformations take place:</p>\n            <ul class='list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1.5 text-slate-300'>\n                <li><strong>Imbibition & Swelling:</strong> The dry seed rapidly absorbs water molecules through a tiny microscopic micropyle pore. This rapid intake of water swells the seed, dramatically increasing its volume.</li>\n                <li><strong>Rupturing the Testa:</strong> The tough outer protective skin of the seed, called the <strong>seed coat</strong> or <em>testa</em>, becomes pliable, softens, and splits open, allowing the emerging radical to break through.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dissolving Stored Food:</strong> Cotyledons store complex starches and proteins. Absorbed water dissolves these reserves, enabling digestive enzymes (amylases and proteases) to convert insoluble starch into soluble sugars that fuel the growing embryo.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Water serves as the chemical catalyst that awakens a sleeping seed. Through a tiny microscopic pore called the micropyle, the seed absorbs water rapidly in a physical process called imbibition. As water floods inside, the dry tissues swell with great force, softening the tough outer seed coat, known as the testa, until it gently splits open. Simultaneously, water dissolves stored insoluble starches within the fleshy cotyledons, activating vital enzymes that convert stored nutrients into simple sugars to nourish the emerging embryo.', 'lecture_script': 'Water serves as the chemical catalyst that awakens a sleeping seed. Through a tiny microscopic pore called the micropyle, the seed absorbs water rapidly in a physical process called imbibition. As water floods inside, the dry tissues swell with great force, softening the tough outer seed coat, known as the testa, until it gently splits open. Simultaneously, water dissolves stored insoluble starches within the fleshy cotyledons, activating vital enzymes that convert stored nutrients into simple sugars to nourish the emerging embryo.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the biological term for the rapid physical uptake of water by dry seeds that causes them to swell and burst their seed coat?', 'options': ['Sublimation', 'Imbibition', 'Transpiration', 'Photosynthesis'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Imbibition is the special type of diffusion where water is absorbed by solid colloids (like dry seed starch and proteins), causing immense swelling pressure.', 'study_insight': 'Imbibition generates sufficient mechanical pressure to crack open hard seed shells and even rock fissures.'}, {'question': 'What crucial biochemical role does water play inside the cotyledons during early seed germination?', 'options': ['It converts leaves into colorful flower petals before roots emerge', 'It activates metabolic enzymes to break down stored insoluble starch into soluble sugars', 'It freezes the embryo cells to preserve them against heat', 'It repels all incoming soil nutrients to keep the seed clean'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Enzymatic hydrolysis requires an aqueous medium; water dissolves stored nutrients and activates enzymes that digest reserve food for the growing seedling.', 'study_insight': 'Stored food in cotyledons sustains the seedling until true green leaves can photosynthesize.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Soil Breathers', 'subtitle': 'Oxygen in Soil Pores, Cellular Respiration & The Danger of Waterlogging', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Can a submerged seed sprout if it is deprived of air? Just like animals, a germinating embryo needs to breathe oxygen to oxidize its stored nutrients into energy (ATP). This critical process is <strong>aerobic respiration</strong>.</p>\n            <div class='space-y-2 mt-2 text-slate-300'>\n                <p><strong>Soil Micro-Cavities:</strong> Healthy garden soil is not a solid block of dirt; it contains tiny gaps and pores between soil particles. These pores are filled with atmospheric air containing roughly 21% oxygen.</p>\n                <p><strong>The Suffocation Experiment:</strong> If seeds are placed at the bottom of a tall glass test tube filled with boiled, cooled water (where dissolved air has been driven out) and covered with a layer of oil to block air, the seeds swell with water but <em>completely fail to sprout</em> and rot!</p>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-500/20 text-xs text-amber-200 mt-2'>\n                    ⚠️ <strong>Agricultural Hazard - Waterlogging:</strong> Overwatering fields fills every soil pore with stagnant water, drowning the roots and suffocating germinating seeds due to complete oxygen starvation.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'While water wakes a sleeping seed, fresh air fuels its growth. Just like animals, developing plant embryos require oxygen for cellular respiration to extract energy from stored carbohydrates. Well-tilled garden soil is rich in microscopic air pockets nestled between mineral particles. When seeds are planted in loose soil, they easily absorb this trapped oxygen. However, if soil becomes waterlogged or if seeds are submerged under boiled water covered with oil, oxygen is completely cut off. Deprived of oxygen, the embryo cannot respire, and instead of sprouting, it suffocates and rots.', 'lecture_script': 'While water wakes a sleeping seed, fresh air fuels its growth. Just like animals, developing plant embryos require oxygen for cellular respiration to extract energy from stored carbohydrates. Well-tilled garden soil is rich in microscopic air pockets nestled between mineral particles. When seeds are planted in loose soil, they easily absorb this trapped oxygen. However, if soil becomes waterlogged or if seeds are submerged under boiled water covered with oil, oxygen is completely cut off. Deprived of oxygen, the embryo cannot respire, and instead of sprouting, it suffocates and rots.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why do bean seeds submerged at the bottom of a beaker filled with boiled, oil-sealed water fail to germinate?', 'options': ['They do not receive sufficient gravity to pull roots downward', 'Boiled water lacks dissolved oxygen essential for cellular respiration', 'Oil turns the seeds into stone within hours', 'Seeds can only germinate when exposed to direct atmospheric wind'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Boiling expels dissolved gases, and an oil seal blocks atmospheric oxygen absorption. Deprived of oxygen, the embryo cannot generate ATP and suffocates.', 'study_insight': 'Germinating seeds require continuous aerobic respiration to fuel active cell multiplication.'}, {'question': 'Why do experienced farmers and gardeners regularly hoe and loosen the topsoil around young crops?', 'options': ['To pack the soil completely solid so water cannot evaporate', 'To aerate the soil pores and supply abundant oxygen to underground roots', 'To prevent earthworms from entering the garden bed', 'To change the color of the soil from brown to yellow'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Plowing and hoeing loosen soil compaction, creating interconnected air pores that supply vital oxygen for root and seedling respiration.', 'study_insight': 'Good soil aeration is just as important for crop health as adequate water and sunlight.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Sun & Shadow', 'subtitle': 'Dark Germination vs. The Crucial Light Dependency of Growing Seedlings', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Do seeds need sunlight to germinate? A classic scientific puzzle emerges when we compare seeds buried beneath inches of dark topsoil with those exposed to bright daylight:</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3'>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-indigo-400 uppercase'>Stage 1: Underground Germination</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>Most seeds (like chickpea, bean, and wheat) do <strong>not require light</strong> to germinate! They are naturally buried deep underground in total darkness. The embryo relies exclusively on energy stored in its fleshy cotyledons.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase'>Stage 2: Post-Emergence Photosynthesis</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>Once stored food in the cotyledons is exhausted, the seedling must synthesize its own food. If kept in dark cupboards, seedlings become pale, spindly, and fragile (etiolated), and quickly wither away. <strong>Sunlight is indispensable for chlorophyll activation and photosynthesis!</strong></p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Do seeds need bright sunlight to sprout? The answer reveals a fascinating two-stage biological sequence. Most garden seeds, such as gram and beans, germinate readily in complete darkness beneath deep soil, nourished entirely by energy stored inside their cotyledons. In fact, total darkness mimics the natural underground environment. However, once the young shoot pushes through the earth into the open air, sunlight becomes utterly essential. Without sunlight, the seedling cannot synthesize green chlorophyll or produce food through photosynthesis, turning pale, weak, and eventually dying.', 'lecture_script': 'Do seeds need bright sunlight to sprout? The answer reveals a fascinating two-stage biological sequence. Most garden seeds, such as gram and beans, germinate readily in complete darkness beneath deep soil, nourished entirely by energy stored inside their cotyledons. In fact, total darkness mimics the natural underground environment. However, once the young shoot pushes through the earth into the open air, sunlight becomes utterly essential. Without sunlight, the seedling cannot synthesize green chlorophyll or produce food through photosynthesis, turning pale, weak, and eventually dying.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Can moong or chickpea seeds germinate successfully if kept inside a dark wooden cupboard with moisture and air?', 'options': ['No, seeds cannot absorb water unless hit by ultraviolet light', 'Yes, early germination relies on stored cotyledon nutrients and does not require light', 'No, darkness permanently poisons the embryo enzymes', 'Yes, but only if an artificial electric heater is placed touching them'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Seed germination under dark conditions proceeds normally because the young embryo metabolizes stored cotyledon food reserves without needing photosynthesis.', 'study_insight': 'Underground seeds are evolutionarily adapted to germinate in the absence of light.'}, {'question': 'What happens to a newly sprouted seedling if it is kept inside a pitch-dark room for two weeks following germination?', 'options': ['It grows thicker, darker green leaves and produces fruit', 'It becomes pale, elongated, and weak, eventually dying of starvation', 'It turns into a non-living mineral crystal', 'It continues healthy growth forever using room air alone'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Without light, the seedling cannot perform photosynthesis to produce new sugars once cotyledon reserves are depleted, resulting in etiolation and starvation.', 'study_insight': 'Light transitions from optional during germination to mandatory during seedling maturation.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Root & Shoot', 'subtitle': 'Activity 10.3: Gravitropism & Phototropism in Sprouting Seedlings', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>How does a buried seed know which direction is 'up' towards the sky and which direction is 'down' towards the Earth? Plants coordinate growth directions through directional growth responses called <strong>tropisms</strong>:</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3'>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 uppercase'>Positive Geotropism (Roots)</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>The embryonic root (<strong>radicle</strong>) always curves downwards into the earth, sensing the pull of gravity and chasing subterranean moisture. Even if you plant a seed upside down, the radicle makes a sharp U-turn towards gravity!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/70 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <h4 class='text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 uppercase'>Positive Phototropism (Shoots)</h4>\n                    <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-1'>The embryonic shoot (<strong>plumule</strong>) always grows upwards against gravity. When placed inside a room near a sunlit window, plant hormones (auxins) accumulate on the shaded side, causing the stem to bend towards the light source!</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-3'>In <strong>Activity 10.3</strong>, rotating a potted bean plant on its side proves that within 24 to 48 hours, the stem bends upward towards the light while the roots curve downward toward the ground.</p>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'How does a seed buried in dark soil know which way is up and which way is down? Plants possess remarkable directional growth responses called tropisms. The emerging root, or radicle, displays positive geotropism, growing relentlessly downward in response to gravity to anchor the plant and absorb moisture. Conversely, the developing shoot, or plumule, exhibits positive phototropism, growing upward toward sunlight. In Activity 10.3, placing a sprouted seedling sideways on a moist tile demonstrates this beautifully: within hours, the root bends downward toward the earth, while the green stem arches gracefully upward toward the light.', 'lecture_script': 'How does a seed buried in dark soil know which way is up and which way is down? Plants possess remarkable directional growth responses called tropisms. The emerging root, or radicle, displays positive geotropism, growing relentlessly downward in response to gravity to anchor the plant and absorb moisture. Conversely, the developing shoot, or plumule, exhibits positive phototropism, growing upward toward sunlight. In Activity 10.3, placing a sprouted seedling sideways on a moist tile demonstrates this beautifully: within hours, the root bends downward toward the earth, while the green stem arches gracefully upward toward the light.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the biological term for the upward bending growth of a green plant stem towards a window providing unidirectional sunlight?', 'options': ['Positive phototropism', 'Negative geotropism exclusively', 'Hydrophobic repulsion', 'Thermonastic crystallization'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Phototropism is directional growth in response to light. Shoots bend toward light (positive phototropism) to maximize solar absorption for photosynthesis.', 'study_insight': 'Auxin hormones cause cells on the shaded side of the stem to elongate faster, bending the plant toward the light.'}, {'question': 'If a germinating bean seed is planted completely upside down with its radicle pointing upward, what path will the emerging root take?', 'options': ['It will continue growing straight up into the air and become a flower', 'It will sense gravity, make a 180° curve, and grow downwards into the soil', 'It will explode due to inverted gravitational forces', 'It will stop growing completely and turn into a rock'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Roots exhibit positive geotropism; specialized gravity-sensing cells (statocytes with statoliths) in the root cap reorient root growth toward gravity.', 'study_insight': 'Plants possess sophisticated sensory systems that detect gravitational acceleration.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'J.C. Bose', 'subtitle': 'Pioneering Indian Biophysicist, Plant Sensitivity & The Crescograph', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>For centuries, the Western scientific consensus regarded plants as passive, unconscious biological machines incapable of sensing pain or environmental disturbances. This dogma was revolutionized by the great Indian polymath and pioneer biophysicist, <strong>Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose (1858–1937)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class='space-y-2 mt-2 text-slate-300'>\n                <p><strong>The Crescograph:</strong> J.C. Bose invented the <em>crescograph</em>, an ingenious instrument utilizing high-precision gear-and-lever optical magnification capable of enlarging plant growth movements by up to <strong>10,000 times</strong>!</p>\n                <p><strong>Electrical Impulses in Flora:</strong> Bose connected sensitive galvanometers to plant tissues, demonstrating that plants generate electrical action potentials identical to animal nerve impulses when stimulated by touch, chemicals, heat, or electric shocks.</p>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-500/30 text-xs text-indigo-200 mt-2'>\n                    🔬 <strong>Bose's Heritage:</strong> J.C. Bose proved that the boundary between animal and plant responsiveness is remarkably porous. His work laid the foundations of plant neurobiology and modern wireless communications.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': "For centuries, plants were considered passive organisms incapable of feeling. This perception was overturned by the visionary Indian scientist, Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose. In the early twentieth century, Bose invented the crescograph, a magnificent device that magnified microscopic plant growth by up to ten thousand times. Using delicate instruments, he proved that plants possess electrical response systems remarkably similar to animal nerves. When exposed to heat, cold, or chemical anesthetics, plants produce measurable electrical pulses and emotional fatigue. Bose's pioneering discoveries opened the world's eyes to the vibrant sensitivity of plant life.", 'lecture_script': "For centuries, plants were considered passive organisms incapable of feeling. This perception was overturned by the visionary Indian scientist, Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose. In the early twentieth century, Bose invented the crescograph, a magnificent device that magnified microscopic plant growth by up to ten thousand times. Using delicate instruments, he proved that plants possess electrical response systems remarkably similar to animal nerves. When exposed to heat, cold, or chemical anesthetics, plants produce measurable electrical pulses and emotional fatigue. Bose's pioneering discoveries opened the world's eyes to the vibrant sensitivity of plant life.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What groundbreaking scientific instrument did Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose invent to record microscopic plant growth?', 'options': ['The astronomical telescope', 'The crescograph', 'The mercury barometer', 'The seismograph'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'J.C. Bose invented the crescograph in the early 20th century, which magnified minute plant movements up to 10,000 times to observe growth and responses.', 'study_insight': "Bose's precision engineering enabled the measurement of growth rates as small as 1/100,000th of an inch per second."}, {'question': 'What revolutionary scientific conclusion did J.C. Bose prove regarding the response of plants to electrical and chemical stimuli?', 'options': ['Plants are completely made of iron and do not conduct electricity', 'Plants generate electrical impulses and show fatigue similar to animal tissues', 'Plants only respond to stimuli when spoken to in English', 'Plants have brains identical in size to human brains'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Bose demonstrated that plant tissues conduct electrical action potentials and exhibit physiological fatigue when repeatedly stimulated, mirroring animal neurobiology.', 'study_insight': 'Bose bridged the perceived gap between plant physiology and animal biophysics.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Plant Cycle', 'subtitle': 'Complete Ontogeny: From Seed Germination to Flowering, Seed Dispersal & Renewal', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>A plant's existence is a continuous biological journey called the <strong>plant life cycle</strong>:</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2 mt-3 text-center'>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400'>1. Seed</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Dormant embryo in protective coat</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400'>2. Seedling</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Radicle roots & plumule green leaves</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400'>3. Vegetative</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Rapid stems, branches & foliage</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400'>4. Flowering</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Reproductive organs bloom</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400'>5. Fruit & Pod</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Pollination yields ripe seed pods</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400'>6. Dispersal</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Wind, water, or animals scatter seeds</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-3'>In annual plants (like mustard, peas, and wheat), the parent plant dies after shedding seeds, while the dispersed seeds ensure the survival of the next generation.</p>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'The life cycle of a flowering plant is a journey of transformation. It begins with a dormant seed awakening in warm, moist soil. The radicle anchors into the earth as roots, while the plumule emerges into the sky as a delicate green seedling. Fueled by photosynthesis, the seedling enters a vigorous vegetative phase, branching outward and unfurling lush foliage. At maturity, it blooms with vibrant flowers, the reproductive organs of the plant. Following pollination and fertilization, flowers mature into seed-bearing pods and fruits. When these seeds are dispersed by wind, water, or animals, the cycle begins anew.', 'lecture_script': 'The life cycle of a flowering plant is a journey of transformation. It begins with a dormant seed awakening in warm, moist soil. The radicle anchors into the earth as roots, while the plumule emerges into the sky as a delicate green seedling. Fueled by photosynthesis, the seedling enters a vigorous vegetative phase, branching outward and unfurling lush foliage. At maturity, it blooms with vibrant flowers, the reproductive organs of the plant. Following pollination and fertilization, flowers mature into seed-bearing pods and fruits. When these seeds are dispersed by wind, water, or animals, the cycle begins anew.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which organ of a mature angiosperm plant houses the reproductive structures responsible for seed formation?', 'options': ['The underground taproot', 'The flower', 'The bark cuticle', 'The leaf petiole'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Flowers are the specialized reproductive shoots of flowering plants containing stamens (male) and carpels/pistils (female) for sexual reproduction.', 'study_insight': 'Pollination transfers pollen from anther to stigma, leading to fertilization and seed development.'}, {'question': 'Why is seed dispersal by wind, water, or animals vital for the long-term survival of plant species?', 'options': ['It ensures seeds land inside the same pot so they stay warm', 'It prevents severe overcrowding, resource competition, and spreads the species across habitats', 'It cools the parent plant during hot summer afternoons', 'It turns seeds into flying insects'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Dispersal spreads offspring away from parent plants, reducing intense competition for sunlight, water, and soil minerals while colonizing new geographic zones.', 'study_insight': 'Without dispersal, seedlings clustered under the mother plant would suffer severe mortality.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Mosquito Four', 'subtitle': 'Complete Metamorphosis: Egg, Wriggler, Tumbler, Adult & Oil Film Vector Control', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Mosquitoes undergo complete metamorphosis consisting of <strong>four distinct life stages</strong>:</p>\n            <div class='space-y-2 mt-2 text-slate-300'>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-5 space-y-1'>\n                    <li><strong>1. Egg:</strong> Laid on stagnant water surfaces (in rafts or singly) in open drains, coolers, old tyres, and flowerpots.</li>\n                    <li><strong>2. Larva (Wriggler):</strong> Hatches in water, wriggles actively, and feeds on microscopic organic matter. It breathes atmospheric air through a specialized <strong>respiratory siphon tube</strong> projected through the water's surface tension film.</li>\n                    <li><strong>3. Pupa (Tumbler):</strong> Comma-shaped, non-feeding stage that rolls and tumbles when disturbed. It breathes through respiratory trumpets piercing the surface film.</li>\n                    <li><strong>4. Adult:</strong> The winged mosquito emerges, dries its wings, and takes flight to spread diseases like malaria, dengue, and chikungunya.</li>\n                </ul>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-500/30 text-xs text-rose-200 mt-2'>\n                    🛢️ <strong>Public Health Science:</strong> Spraying a thin film of kerosene or oil on stagnant water lowers surface tension and physically seals the water surface. Larval breathing siphons cannot penetrate the oil slick, suffocating wrigglers and pupae before they can become biting adults!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': 'Mosquitoes undergo complete metamorphosis through four distinct stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. Female mosquitoes lay eggs upon the surface of stagnant water. These hatch into active larvae, commonly known as wrigglers. Wrigglers hang upside down just beneath the surface film, breathing air through a narrow respiratory siphon tube. Soon, they transform into comma-shaped pupae, called tumblers, before emerging as winged adult mosquitoes. To control mosquito populations and prevent dangerous diseases like malaria and dengue, a thin layer of kerosene or oil is sprayed over stagnant water. The oil film forms an airtight barrier, suffocating both wrigglers and pupae by blocking their breathing siphons.', 'lecture_script': 'Mosquitoes undergo complete metamorphosis through four distinct stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. Female mosquitoes lay eggs upon the surface of stagnant water. These hatch into active larvae, commonly known as wrigglers. Wrigglers hang upside down just beneath the surface film, breathing air through a narrow respiratory siphon tube. Soon, they transform into comma-shaped pupae, called tumblers, before emerging as winged adult mosquitoes. To control mosquito populations and prevent dangerous diseases like malaria and dengue, a thin layer of kerosene or oil is sprayed over stagnant water. The oil film forms an airtight barrier, suffocating both wrigglers and pupae by blocking their breathing siphons.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How does pouring a thin layer of kerosene or motor oil onto stagnant pond water eradicate mosquito larvae and pupae?', 'options': ['It feeds the larvae so much that they burst from overeating', 'It blocks the surface water tension, suffocating the breathing siphons of wrigglers and pupae', 'It turns the water into solid ice within seconds', 'It makes the water taste sweet, attracting predatory fish'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Oil forms a floating surface slick that prevents larval siphons and pupal trumpets from accessing atmospheric oxygen, resulting in suffocation.', 'study_insight': "Physical vector control disrupts the mosquito's mechanical breathing interface."}, {'question': "Which developmental stage of the mosquito life cycle is actively mobile and referred to as a 'wriggler'?", 'options': ['The egg stage', 'The larval stage', 'The adult winged stage', 'The dormant seed stage'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The larva is commonly called a wriggler due to its rhythmic flexions while moving underwater and filtering organic debris.', 'study_insight': 'Wrigglers are aquatic but strictly obligate air-breathers.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Silk Cocoon', 'subtitle': 'The Silk Moth Cycle: Caterpillar Silkworm, Cocoon Spinning & Sericulture Heritage', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>The story of silk weaves biological wonder with India's rich handloom heritage:</p>\n            <div class='space-y-2 mt-2 text-slate-300'>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-5 space-y-1'>\n                    <li><strong>1. Egg:</strong> Female silk moth (<em>Bombyx mori</em>) lays hundreds of tiny eggs on fresh mulberry leaves.</li>\n                    <li><strong>2. Silkworm Larva (Caterpillar):</strong> Hatches and feeds ravenously day and night on tender mulberry foliage, molting four times as its size multiplies exponentially.</li>\n                    <li><strong>3. Pupa & Cocoon:</strong> When fully grown, the caterpillar weaves a protective nest. It moves its head in figure-eight patterns, extruding a liquid protein (fibroin and sericin) from salivary glands that hardens upon contact with air into a continuous <strong>silk filament</strong> up to 1,000 meters long! The caterpillar encases itself inside this oval protective shell called a <strong>cocoon</strong>.</li>\n                    <li><strong>4. Adult Silk Moth:</strong> The pupa undergoes metamorphosis inside the cocoon and emerges as a flightless adult moth.</li>\n                </ul>\n                <div class='p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 text-xs text-amber-200 mt-2'>\n                    🧵 <strong>Indian Sericulture Heritage:</strong> Rearing silkworms for commercial silk production is called <strong>sericulture</strong>, championed across rural India by the Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC), supporting millions of traditional weavers.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ", 'spoken_text': "The silk moth cycle showcases nature's master weavers. A female moth deposits hundreds of tiny eggs on mulberry leaves. Tiny caterpillars hatch and feast voraciously on the green leaves, molting multiple times as they grow rapidly. When mature, the caterpillar swings its head in elegant figure-eight movements, secreting a liquid protein that hardens in the air into a shimmering thread of natural silk. Wrapping this continuous thread around itself, it creates a cozy protective fortress called a cocoon, inside which it transforms into a pupa. In India, the rearing of silkworms is known as sericulture, a cherished rural cottage craft fostered by the Khadi and Village Industries Commission.", 'lecture_script': "The silk moth cycle showcases nature's master weavers. A female moth deposits hundreds of tiny eggs on mulberry leaves. Tiny caterpillars hatch and feast voraciously on the green leaves, molting multiple times as they grow rapidly. When mature, the caterpillar swings its head in elegant figure-eight movements, secreting a liquid protein that hardens in the air into a shimmering thread of natural silk. Wrapping this continuous thread around itself, it creates a cozy protective fortress called a cocoon, inside which it transforms into a pupa. In India, the rearing of silkworms is known as sericulture, a cherished rural cottage craft fostered by the Khadi and Village Industries Commission.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What primary food do silkworm caterpillars consume continuously to gain energy for spinning silk cocoons?', 'options': ['Fresh mulberry leaves', 'Grains of polished white rice', 'Pollen from sunflower blooms', 'Sand grains from riverbanks'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Silkworms (larvae of Bombyx mori) feed exclusively on tender mulberry leaves, which supply the amino acids required for synthesizing silk protein.', 'study_insight': 'Dietary protein from mulberry leaves is directly converted into fibroin silk filaments.'}, {'question': 'At which stage of the silk moth life cycle is the valuable continuous commercial silk filament harvested?', 'options': ['Directly from freshly laid unfertilized eggs', 'From the woven protective cocoon surrounding the pupa', 'From the wings of the flying adult moth', 'From the roots of the mulberry tree'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Commercial silk is harvested from the intact cocoon, which is unraveled to yield a continuous natural silk filament up to 1,000 meters in length.', 'study_insight': 'Sericulture combines biological husbandry with traditional rural textile craftsmanship.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Frog Jump', 'subtitle': 'Amphibian Metamorphosis: Spawn, Aquatic Tadpole with Gills, Froglet & Adult', 'concept_html': "\n            <p>Frogs present one of the most astonishing transformations in the animal kingdom—a journey from fully aquatic fish-like herbivore to terrestrial leaping carnivore called <strong>metamorphosis</strong>:</p>\n            <div class='grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2 mt-3 text-center'>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-sky-400'>1. Spawn</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Clumps of jelly-coated eggs laid in ponds</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-sky-400'>2. Tadpole</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Limbless, swims with tail, breathes via gills</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400'>3. Froglet</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Hind & front legs sprout; tail reabsorbed</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class='p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800'>\n                    <div class='text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400'>4. Adult Frog</div>\n                    <p class='text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-0.5'>Tailless, breathes via lungs & moist skin</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mt-3'>Notice the profound anatomical shifts: the vegetarian tadpole has an elongated intestine and gills; the carnivorous adult possesses muscular jumping legs, sticky projectile tongue, internal lungs, and cutaneous skin respiration.</p>\n        ", 'spoken_text': "The life cycle of a frog is one of nature's greatest transformations, known as metamorphosis. The female frog lays clusters of jelly-cushioned eggs called spawn in quiet ponds. From these eggs emerge swimming tadpoles that resemble small fish, possessing long tails and breathing dissolved oxygen through gills. As the tadpole matures, incredible changes occur: hind legs sprout, followed by front limbs. Its swimming tail is gradually reabsorbed into the body, internal gills are replaced by lungs, and its digestive system adapts for an insect-catching diet. Finally, the mature adult frog hops onto land, capable of breathing through both lungs and moist skin.", 'lecture_script': "The life cycle of a frog is one of nature's greatest transformations, known as metamorphosis. The female frog lays clusters of jelly-cushioned eggs called spawn in quiet ponds. From these eggs emerge swimming tadpoles that resemble small fish, possessing long tails and breathing dissolved oxygen through gills. As the tadpole matures, incredible changes occur: hind legs sprout, followed by front limbs. Its swimming tail is gradually reabsorbed into the body, internal gills are replaced by lungs, and its digestive system adapts for an insect-catching diet. Finally, the mature adult frog hops onto land, capable of breathing through both lungs and moist skin.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which respiratory organ allows a young aquatic frog tadpole to extract dissolved oxygen from pond water?', 'options': ['Paired air-filled terrestrial lungs', 'Delicate aquatic gills', 'A hollow tracheal siphon tube', 'Feathered aerial nostrils'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Early tadpoles are completely aquatic and rely on external and internal gills to extract dissolved oxygen from water, much like fish.', 'study_insight': 'Metamorphosis involves comprehensive anatomical restructuring of respiratory, circulatory, and digestive systems.'}, {'question': 'What happens to the long swimming tail of a tadpole as it completes its transformation into an adult frog?', 'options': ['It snaps off in the water and swims away as a new eel', "Its tissues and proteins are enzymatically broken down and reabsorbed into the froglet's body", 'It turns into a hard protective shell on its back', 'It falls off and decomposes on the muddy pond bottom'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Programmed cell death (apoptosis) and enzymatic histolysis break down the tail tissues, reabsorbing its valuable nutrients to fuel the growth of limbs and lungs.', 'study_insight': 'Biological metamorphosis efficiently recycles larval organs into adult structures.'}]}]

QUIZ_QUESTIONS = [{'id': 1, 'question': 'Which of the following is a non-negotiable biological characteristic shared by ALL living organisms without exception?', 'options': ['Possessing red blood and four walking limbs', 'Undergoing metabolic respiration, cellular growth, and responsiveness to stimuli', 'Flying through the air to capture flying insects', 'Living exclusively submerged under oceanic water'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'While specialized traits like walking limbs or flying vary, all living beings must respire, grow, excrete, and respond to stimuli at a cellular level.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Locomotion vs. Life:</strong> Many living organisms (such as rooted trees, corals, and fungi) do not possess walking limbs or fly.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Core Characteristics:</strong> The universal criteria that define living organisms across all kingdoms are: cellular organization, nutrition, cellular respiration, metabolic excretion, growth, irritability (response to stimuli), reproduction, and death.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B captures the universal biological processes shared by all living entities.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 2, 'question': 'Why is an empty spiral snail shell found on garden soil considered a non-living remnant rather than an active living organism?', 'options': ['Because it is made purely of plastic manufactured in factories', 'Because without the living snail, the shell does not respire, grow, eat, or reproduce', 'Because living things never have any calcium in their bodies', 'Because snails are mythical creatures that do not truly exist'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'An empty shell is an organic-calcium structure produced by a living organism, but on its own it lacks cellular metabolism, respiration, and self-repair.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Biological Origin:</strong> The shell was produced by the mantle tissue of a living snail as an exoskeleton.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Status of the Abandoned Shell:</strong> Once the organism dies or abandons the shell, the remaining calcium carbonate casing has no living cells, conducts no metabolism, consumes no food, and cannot reproduce.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Hence, the isolated shell is classified as a non-living remnant.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 3, 'question': 'How do plants exhibit biological movement despite being anchored firmly into the soil by their root systems?', 'options': ['By lifting their roots into the air and walking across fields at midnight', 'Through directional tropisms such as stems bending to sunlight and tendrils coiling around supports', 'Plants never exhibit any form of movement under any circumstances', 'By shrinking their total volume to zero during cold winter nights'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Plants do not exhibit whole-body locomotion, but they display active differential growth movements (tropisms), opening/closing of flowers, and coiling of tendrils.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Locomotion vs. Movement:</strong> Locomotion is the movement of an entire organism from one place to another (common in animals). Movement refers to the change in posture, orientation, or shape of organs.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Botanical Movements:</strong> Examples include phototropism (stems bending towards light), hydrotropism (roots seeking water), thigmotropism (tendrils coiling upon touch), and nastic movements (sleep movements of amla leaves and Mimosa folding).</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B accurately highlights plant-specific biological movements.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 4, 'question': 'What is the specialized prey-trapping mechanism employed by the carnivorous plant Drosera (Sundew)?', 'options': ['It digs underground pitfall traps lined with spiderwebs', 'Its leaves possess glandular tentacles tipped with sticky glistening droplets that curl around trapped insects', 'It fires toxic spines from a distance of three meters', 'It emits ultrasonic sounds that stun bees mid-flight'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Drosera leaves are covered with mucilage-secreting glandular hairs (tentacles). When an insect sticks to the mucilage, the tentacles bend inward to trap and digest it.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Morphology:</strong> The sundew (<em>Drosera</em>) grows in nutrient-poor acidic soils and supplements its nitrogen needs by capturing insects.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Trapping Mechanism:</strong> Glandular hairs secrete sticky, glistening mucilage drops resembling morning dew. Contact with an insect stimulates thigmonastic bending of surrounding hairs within seconds.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Digestion:</strong> Digestive enzymes break down the insect's soft tissues into absorbable nitrogen compounds.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 5, 'question': 'Which statement correctly distinguishes physical expansion in non-living objects from biological growth in living organisms?', 'options': ['Biological growth occurs by internal cellular division and synthesis of protoplasm, whereas non-living growth is external accumulation', 'Non-living growth requires water and oxygen, while animals grow without absorbing any matter', 'Biological growth can be reversed by rubbing the animal with a towel', 'There is no physical or biological difference between the two'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': "Living growth is intrinsic (from within) and irreversible, driven by cell division and metabolism. Non-living 'growth' (like a sand dune or crystal) is extrinsic accretion of surface matter.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Intrinsic Growth:</strong> In living beings, food is ingested, digested, and converted into cellular structures via mitosis, increasing size permanently.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Extrinsic Growth:</strong> A crystal growing in a supersaturated solution or a sand dune expanding in the desert simply accumulates existing particles on its exterior surface.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A describes the exact scientific difference.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 6, 'question': 'What is the primary difference between physical breathing and cellular respiration?', 'options': ['Breathing occurs inside mitochondria while respiration occurs in the nose', 'Breathing is the mechanical exchange of gases, whereas respiration is the biochemical breakdown of glucose to release energy', 'Breathing produces food while respiration destroys water', 'Only fish respire while land animals exclusively breathe'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Breathing is the physical ventilation process (inhaling oxygen, exhaling carbon dioxide). Cellular respiration is the chemical oxidation of nutrients yielding ATP energy.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Mechanical Breathing:</strong> Involves physical respiratory organs (lungs, gills, trachea) drawing in environmental oxygen and venting carbon dioxide.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Biochemical Respiration:</strong> Occurs within every living cell (cytoplasm and mitochondria): Glucose + Oxygen ➔ Carbon Dioxide + Water + Usable Chemical Energy (ATP).</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the accurate distinction.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 7, 'question': 'Through which microscopic structures do plant leaves exchange gases during cellular respiration and photosynthesis?', 'options': ['Stomata regulated by pairs of guard cells', 'Root cap mucilage vesicles', 'Petal pigment chromoplasts', 'Xylem tracheid pits'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Stomata are microscopic pores located primarily on the lower surface of leaves. Guard cells swell or shrink to open or close the stoma, facilitating gas exchange.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Structure of Stomata:</strong> Each stoma is an elliptical aperture enclosed by two specialized, chlorophyll-containing guard cells.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Function:</strong> They permit oxygen uptake and carbon dioxide release during respiration, as well as carbon dioxide intake and oxygen release during daytime photosynthesis.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is correct.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 8, 'question': 'Why do earthworms surface from their underground burrows onto open soil during heavy monsoon rainstorms?', 'options': ['Because rainwater fills soil air pores, causing hypoxia and suffocation underground', 'Because earthworms require direct sunlight to tan their outer cuticle', 'Because they feed exclusively on falling raindrops', 'Because earthworms cannot crawl when soil is moist'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Earthworms breathe cutaneously by absorbing oxygen dissolved in the film of moisture on their skin from soil air pores. When soil is flooded, air is displaced, forcing worms to surface for air.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Cutaneous Respiration:</strong> Earthworms lack lungs and gills; oxygen diffuses directly through their moist, capillary-rich outer cuticle into their bloodstream.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Effect of Waterlogging:</strong> When heavy rains saturate the soil, water replaces all air in subterranean tunnels. Without oxygen, earthworms must emerge to the surface to breathe.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A accurately explains this ecological observation.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 9, 'question': 'Which of the following processes is a true example of metabolic excretion in living plants?', 'options': ['Absorbing water and mineral salts through root hairs', 'Guttation of excess water and dissolved mineral salts from leaf margins, and shedding waste in autumn leaves', 'Producing glucose in chloroplasts during daytime photosynthesis', 'Opening flower petals in response to morning sunlight'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Excretion involves the elimination of metabolic wastes. Plants excrete excess water and salts via guttation hydathodes and store wastes in leaves that drop seasonally.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Definition of Excretion:</strong> The biological removal of metabolic waste products from the body.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Botanical Strategies:</strong> Plants eliminate wastes through hydathodes (guttation droplets), bark shedding, and leaf abscission where accumulated mineral wastes are discarded.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B represents plant excretory mechanisms.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 10, 'question': 'What is the biological significance of sweat production in humans during strenuous exercise or hot summer afternoons?', 'options': ['It produces new muscle tissue directly on the skin surface', 'It eliminates metabolic wastes (water, urea, salts) and cools the body via evaporative cooling', 'It turns excess body fat directly into atmospheric nitrogen', 'It prevents skin from absorbing necessary atmospheric oxygen'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Sweating serves dual functions: excretory elimination of excess water, salts, and traces of urea, and thermoregulatory cooling through the latent heat of evaporation.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Excretory Role:</strong> Sweat glands filter interstitial fluid, discharging water, sodium chloride, and metabolic waste traces onto the epidermal surface.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Thermoregulatory Role:</strong> As sweat evaporates, it absorbs latent heat of vaporization from the skin, effectively cooling core body temperature.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the complete biological rationale.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 11, 'question': "What is a 'stimulus' in biological science?", 'options': ['A non-living stone that never changes', 'Any environmental change or event that evokes an active physiological or behavioral response from an organism', 'The permanent death of an entire biological species', 'A microscopic seed that cannot absorb water'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "A stimulus is any detectable change in an organism's external or internal environment (light, heat, sound, touch, chemical) that triggers a response.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Definition:</strong> In physiology, a stimulus is an energetic change in the physical or chemical environment that excites sensory receptors.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Examples:</strong> Bright light hitting the eye, the prick of a thorn on a fingertip, or the scent of food detecting by a hungry animal.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B defines a biological stimulus accurately.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 12, 'question': 'When you accidentally step on a sharp thorn, your foot jerks upward before you consciously register the pain. What is this rapid reaction called?', 'options': ['A reflex action', 'Voluntary muscle fatigue', 'Cellular photosynthesis', 'Imbibition of water'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'A reflex action is an automatic, involuntary, and rapid neural response to a dangerous stimulus, routed through the spinal cord to protect the body from severe injury.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Reflex Arc:</strong> The sensory receptor detects the thorn, transmits an impulse along sensory neurons to the spinal cord, which immediately fires a motor neuron causing leg flexor muscles to contract.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Speed Advantage:</strong> By bypassing the cognitive processing of the brain, the reflex action prevents deeper tissue puncture.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the correct physiological term.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 13, 'question': 'Why do the leaflets of the Chhui-mui (Mimosa pudica) plant fold shut rapidly when lightly touched?', 'options': ['Muscles in the stem contract just like animal arm muscles', 'Water rapidly exits the cells of the swollen leaf base (pulvinus), causing an immediate loss of turgor pressure', 'The leaves dry out and die permanently upon the touch of human skin', 'The plant generates flames to scorch touching fingers'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Mechanical touch triggers an electrical action potential that causes potassium ions and water to rush out of the pulvinus cells at the base of leaflets, collapsing them.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Pulvinus Anatomy:</strong> The swollen hinge-like joint at the base of each petiole and leaflet is called the pulvinus.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Turgor Dynamics:</strong> Touch generates an electrical wave. Cells in the lower half of the pulvinus rapidly pump out potassium and chloride ions, followed by osmotic water loss.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Folding:</strong> Without internal fluid pressure (turgor), the leaf structure droops and leaflets fold together.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 14, 'question': "What ecological or biological advantage does the Amla tree gain from its 'sleeping leaves' (nyctinasty) closing at sunset?", 'options': ['It allows the tree to capture nocturnal moonlight for boiling sap', 'It conserves leaf moisture, protects delicate tissues from nocturnal cold, and reduces surface exposure to pests', 'It turns the leaves into poisonous fruits overnight', 'It allows the tree to walk to another garden during the night'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Nyctinastic sleep movements reduce convective heat loss and nocturnal transpiration, while shielding delicate foliage from nocturnal herbivores and cold.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Nyctinasty:</strong> Circadian rhythm-driven closing of leaves during dark periods.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Adaptive Value:</strong> At night, photosynthesis cannot occur. Folding leaflets reduces exposed surface area, retaining warmth and limiting water vapor loss.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B explains the physiological benefits.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 15, 'question': 'Why is the biological process of reproduction considered essential for the survival of a species, even though an individual can survive without having offspring?', 'options': ['Reproduction keeps an individual organism alive forever without eating', 'Individual organisms eventually die, so reproduction ensures that new generations replace them, preventing species extinction', 'Reproduction changes plants into mammals within one year', 'Reproduction prevents rainwater from entering lakes'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Reproduction is not essential for the individual organism's survival, but it is indispensable for the continuity and survival of the species across generations.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Individual vs. Population:</strong> A sterile animal can live a full, healthy life. However, if an entire population ceases to reproduce, no new individuals are born.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Extinction Prevention:</strong> As existing members succumb to predation, disease, or aging, the species would vanish without reproduction.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the accurate evolutionary explanation.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 16, 'question': 'Which of the following organisms reproduces by laying eggs (oviparous) rather than giving birth to live young?', 'options': ['Cow', 'Pigeon', 'Elephant', 'Human'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Pigeons (and birds in general) are oviparous; they lay hard-shelled eggs that are incubated until chicks hatch. Cows, elephants, and humans are viviparous mammals.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Ovipary:</strong> Embryo develops outside the mother's body within an egg protected by a shell (birds, reptiles, amphibians, insects, fish).</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Vivipary:</strong> Embryo develops directly inside the female uterus, receiving nourishment via the placenta, followed by live birth (mammals).</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Pigeons are oviparous birds (Option B).</p>\n        "}, {'id': 17, 'question': "What is the biological definition of 'death' in living organisms?", 'options': ['A temporary sleep from which an animal awakens after ten minutes', 'The irreversible, permanent cessation of all metabolic processes, cellular repair, and vital functions in an organism', 'The seasonal loss of leaves in deciduous trees during winter', 'The movement of a seed through the digestive tract of a bird'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Death is the permanent, irreversible cessation of all biological functions that sustain a living organism, marking the terminal phase of its lifespan.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Lifespan Trajectory:</strong> Every living creature traverses a defined ontogenetic arc: birth ➔ growth ➔ maturity ➔ senescence ➔ death.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Irreversibility:</strong> Unlike dormancy or hibernation (which are reversible), death involves the permanent breakdown of cellular homeostasis and metabolic pathways.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the rigorous scientific definition.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 18, 'question': 'In Activity 10.2, what are the three indispensable environmental factors tested for successful seed germination?', 'options': ['Direct sunlight, motor oil, and table salt', 'Water (moisture), oxygen (air), and suitable moderate temperature', 'Continuous darkness, boiling water, and high vacuum', 'Chemical pesticides, chemical dyes, and freezing ice'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Seed germination requires moisture to hydrate tissues and soften the coat, oxygen for aerobic cellular respiration, and favorable warmth for enzymatic action.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Moisture:</strong> Activates hydrolytic enzymes and softens the testa.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Air (Oxygen):</strong> Drives aerobic ATP generation in embryo cells.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Temperature:</strong> Most seeds have an optimum range of 20°C to 30°C where enzymes function at maximum velocity.</p>\n            <p>4. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B summarizes the three vital requirements.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 19, 'question': 'What happens if dry gram seeds are placed inside a deep freezer kept at -15°C with water and air present?', 'options': ['They germinate twice as fast as seeds at room temperature', 'They fail to germinate because extreme cold inactivates the metabolic enzymes necessary for embryo growth', 'They turn into adult green plants with ripe pods in one day', 'They absorb ice and start walking around the freezer'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Enzymes operate within specific thermal boundaries. At sub-zero temperatures, water freezes and enzymes are immobilized, preventing germination.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Enzyme Kinetics:</strong> Biochemical reactions of germination require thermal energy. At -15°C, kinetic energy is too low for enzymes to catalyze reactions.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Water Freezing:</strong> Frozen water cannot be transported osmotically into seed tissues.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> The seeds remain dormant or suffer frost damage (Option B).</p>\n        "}, {'id': 20, 'question': "What is the specialized role of the tiny microscopic pore called the 'micropyle' located on the seed coat?", 'options': ['It serves as a chimney to exhaust green smoke during the night', 'It allows water and oxygen to enter the dry seed during imbibition and provides an exit point for the emerging radicle', 'It traps flying beetles inside the seed coat to act as food', 'It absorbs radio waves from the surrounding environment'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The micropyle is the tiny opening through which pollen entered during fertilization, and through which water and oxygen enter during seed germination.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Anatomy:</strong> The micropyle is a minute pore in the testa (seed coat) of an angiosperm seed.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Functional Role:</strong> During soaking, water enters rapidly through the micropyle into the cotyledons. During germination, the embryonic root (radicle) pushes out through this same aperture.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B accurately specifies its dual role.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 21, 'question': 'What is the biological term for the rapid, physical absorption of water by solid dry colloids (like dry seed starch and proteins) that causes immense swelling?', 'options': ['Imbibition', 'Transpiration', 'Sublimation', 'Photosynthesis'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Imbibition is the adsorption of water molecules by hydrophilic colloidal substances within seeds, generating massive hydrostatic swelling pressure.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Colloidal Chemistry:</strong> Dry proteins, cellulose, and starch in seed cotyledons have strong negative electrical affinities for polar water molecules.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Imbibition Pressure:</strong> The swelling pressure generated can exceed hundreds of atmospheres, easily rupturing the toughest seed coats.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the precise botanical term.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 22, 'question': 'Why do seeds soaked in water swell and soften their tough outer coat (testa)?', 'options': ['Because water molecules hydrate the dried cellulose matrix, increasing its elasticity and volume', 'Because the seed fills with air bubbles that expand under pressure', 'Because seeds consume water to build iron armour', 'Because water turns the seed into pure sugar crystals'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Water molecules infiltrate the dense intermolecular spaces of the dry seed coat and cotyledons, hydrating cellular polymers and causing structural expansion and softening.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Hydration Effect:</strong> Dry seed coats are brittle and impermeable. Water hydration loosens intermolecular bonds in the testa, making it pliable and permeable.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Facilitating Emergence:</strong> The softened coat allows the delicate radicle to pierce through without tearing or blunt trauma.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A correctly explains the biophysical mechanism.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 23, 'question': 'Why does covering the water surface with an oil layer in a seed submersion experiment prevent seeds from sprouting?', 'options': ['Oil turns the water into boiling acid', 'The oil layer acts as a physical barrier preventing atmospheric oxygen from dissolving into the water', 'Oil attracts hungry aquatic birds that eat the seeds', 'Oil blocks gravitational forces from pulling the roots down'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Oil is immiscible and floats on water, sealing the liquid surface against atmospheric gas exchange. The seeds consume any residual oxygen and then suffocate.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Experimental Setup:</strong> Boiled water expels dissolved oxygen. Adding an oil layer on top prevents fresh oxygen from redissolving from the atmosphere.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Oxygen Starvation:</strong> The embryo cannot perform aerobic respiration, halting ATP production and preventing cell division in the radicle.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B is the scientific reason.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 24, 'question': 'What is the biological state called when healthy seeds kept in a dry environment remain alive but metabolically dormant for months or years?', 'options': ['Seed dormancy', 'Cellular necrosis', 'Permanent extinction', 'Active pollination'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Seed dormancy is an evolutionary adaptation that arrests embryo development in unfavorable conditions (dryness/cold), preserving viability until favorable cues return.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Suspended Animation:</strong> In dry seeds, cellular metabolic rates drop to less than 1% of normal levels, minimizing nutrient consumption.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Survival Strategy:</strong> This prevents seeds from sprouting during harsh droughts or freezing winters when young seedlings would surely perish.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the correct biological term.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 25, 'question': 'How do seedlings grown in total darkness (etiolated) differ morphologically from seedlings grown in natural sunlight?', 'options': ['They are short, thick, and possess deep purple flowers', 'They become abnormally elongated, pale yellowish-white, and fragile due to lack of chlorophyll synthesis', 'They develop giant roots and zero stems', 'They turn into wooden branches within two hours'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'In darkness, seedlings undergo etiolation: stems rapidly elongate searching for light, while chlorophyll production is blocked, leaving them pale yellow and frail.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Etiolation Syndrome:</strong> Without light cues, the plant allocates all resources to rapid stem elongation to pierce through what it perceives as deep soil.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Chlorophyll Block:</strong> Light is required to convert protochlorophyllide into chlorophyll; without it, plastids remain etioplasts containing yellow carotenoids.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B describes the classic etiolated appearance.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 26, 'question': 'Which directional growth movement causes the roots of a germinating seedling to grow downwards into the soil in response to gravity?', 'options': ['Positive geotropism (gravitropism)', 'Negative phototropism exclusively', 'Positive hydrotropism only', 'Nastic leaflet folding'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Positive geotropism (or gravitropism) is the directional growth of roots towards the pull of gravity, ensuring deep soil anchoring and water uptake.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Gravity Sensing:</strong> Dense starch grains called statoliths in root cap cells settle at the bottom of cells under gravity.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Hormonal Response:</strong> This triggers asymmetric distribution of the growth hormone auxin, which in roots inhibits cell elongation on the lower side, curving the root tip downwards.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the correct botanical classification.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 27, 'question': 'When a potted plant is placed near a window inside a room, why does its green stem bend towards the sunlight?', 'options': ['The stem is fleeing from room furniture', 'Auxin hormones accumulate on the shaded side of the stem, causing those cells to elongate faster and curve the shoot toward light', 'Sunlight pulls the stem mechanically using magnetic forces', 'The leaves on the sunny side become heavy and drag the stem down'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Phototropism is driven by auxin redistribution. Light causes auxins to migrate to the shaded side of the stem, stimulating greater cell elongation there and bending the shoot toward light.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Auxin Redistribution:</strong> When light strikes a plant unilaterally, auxin moves laterally from the irradiated side to the shaded side.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Differential Growth:</strong> In stems, higher auxin concentrations accelerate cell elongation. As the shaded side expands faster than the lit side, the stem curves toward the light.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the exact physiological mechanism.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 28, 'question': "What remarkable capability did Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose's invention, the crescograph, offer to the scientific world?", 'options': ['It allowed scientists to listen to radio stations on Mars', 'It magnified minute plant growth and physiological movements by up to 10,000 times using optical levers', 'It transformed dry desert sand into fertile black soil', 'It predicted monsoon rainfall six years in advance'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "J.C. Bose's crescograph used a compound system of mechanical levers and optical mirrors to magnify tiny plant movements up to 10,000 times, demonstrating plant responsiveness.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Engineering Marvel:</strong> Plant growth is imperceptibly slow (fractions of a millimeter per hour). Direct observation was impossible with ordinary microscopes.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Optical Crescograph:</strong> Bose linked a delicate probe on the growing plant tip to a dual-lever system that recorded deflections onto a smoked glass plate, magnifying movements by up to 10,000×.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B describes Bose's historic breakthrough.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 29, 'question': 'What major physiological discovery did J.C. Bose prove regarding the response of plants to anesthetics like chloroform and ether?', 'options': ['Plants grew wooden legs and ran away', 'Plants became anesthetized, losing electrical responsiveness and growth, and revived when fresh air was restored', 'Plants exploded with loud popping sounds', 'Plants showed zero change because chemicals cannot affect plant cells'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Bose exposed plants to chloroform vapor, demonstrating that plant electrical pulsations ceased under anesthesia, exactly like an anesthetized animal, and resumed upon ventilation.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Comparative Biophysics:</strong> Bose sought to demonstrate unity in biological matter. He subjected plants to animal anesthetics (chloroform, ether).</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Electrical Quenching:</strong> The crescograph and galvanometer showed complete cessation of electrical action potentials during exposure, followed by full recovery upon aeration.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B summarizes Bose's groundbreaking experimental proof.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 30, 'question': 'Which of the following stages correctly outlines the chronological life cycle of a flowering plant?', 'options': ['Adult tree ➔ Seedling ➔ Flower ➔ Seed', 'Seed ➔ Germination & Seedling ➔ Vegetative Growth ➔ Flowering ➔ Fruit & Seed Formation ➔ Dispersal', 'Fruit ➔ Flower ➔ Trunk ➔ Underground radicle', 'Pollen ➔ Seedling ➔ Root hair ➔ Rotten wood'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The plant life cycle proceeds sequentially: dormant seed ➔ germination ➔ vegetative maturation ➔ flowering ➔ fruit/seed development ➔ seed dispersal.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Germination:</strong> Imbibition wakes the embryo, giving rise to radicle and plumule.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Vegetative Stage:</strong> Formation of roots, stems, and photosynthetic foliage.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Reproductive Stage:</strong> Flowering, pollination, double fertilization, fruit formation, and seed dispersal completing the generational loop.</p>\n            <p>4. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B represents the chronological sequence.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 31, 'question': "Which developmental stage of the mosquito life cycle is commonly referred to as a 'tumbler'?", 'options': ['The egg raft', 'The pupal stage', 'The winged adult', 'The crawling caterpillar'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The mosquito pupa is called a tumbler because of its tumbling motion through water when disturbed. The larva is called a wriggler.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Larva (Wriggler):</strong> Moves with rhythmic serpentine wriggling motions and actively feeds on micro-algae.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Pupa (Tumbler):</strong> Comma-shaped, non-feeding stage that rolls and tumbles rapidly towards the pond bottom when shadow or movement threatens it.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> The pupal stage is the tumbler (Option B).</p>\n        "}, {'id': 32, 'question': 'Why do mosquito larvae (wrigglers) hang upside down just beneath the surface film of stagnant pond water?', 'options': ['To sunbathe their transparent tails in the afternoon sun', 'To project their respiratory siphon tube through the surface tension film into atmospheric air to breathe', 'To catch flying honeybees with their tail appendages', 'To avoid swimming near muddy bottom vegetation'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Wrigglers lack aquatic gills; they possess a specialized breathing siphon tube located at their posterior end that must pierce the water's surface film to breathe air.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Respiratory Anatomy:</strong> The mosquito larva is an aquatic insect that breathes atmospheric oxygen via a posterior siphon tube ringed with hydrofuge hairs.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Posture:</strong> By hanging upside down, its mouth brushes sweep food from water currents while the siphon tip stays dry and open to ambient air.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B is the biological explanation.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 33, 'question': 'How does pouring kerosene or eco-friendly larvicidal oil onto stagnant drains eradicate mosquito breeding?', 'options': ['It turns the wrigglers into frogs within five minutes', 'The oil forms an impenetrable floating film that blocks the breathing siphons of larvae and pupae, suffocating them', 'It raises the water temperature to boiling point instantly', 'It makes the water taste bitter so mosquitoes fly away to another country'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Oil spreads across the water surface, lowering surface tension and sealing the air-water interface so breathing siphons cannot draw air, suffocating the aquatic stages.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Surface Tension Disruption:</strong> A monomolecular or thin hydrocarbon oil slick spreads rapidly across the surface of stagnant pools.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Mechanical Suffocation:</strong> Larval siphons and pupal breathing trumpets rely on surface tension to stay unflooded. The oil enters and clogs the siphon tubes, cutting off oxygen and suffocating them.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B describes this effective physical vector control method.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 34, 'question': 'What is the primary commercial product derived from the protective cocoon stage of the silk moth (Bombyx mori)?', 'options': ['Natural silk filament', 'Synthetic polyester fiber', 'Cotton lint batting', 'Jute twine rope'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The cocoon of the silkworm consists of a continuous natural protein filament (fibroin bound by sericin) up to 1,000 meters long, which is harvested and reeled to produce natural silk.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Cocoon Formation:</strong> The mature silkworm extrudes liquid protein from salivary sericteries, weaving an elliptical shell around itself to protect the transforming pupa.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Reeling Silk:</strong> In sericulture, cocoons are processed in warm water to soften the sericin gum and unwind the continuous silk filaments into yarn.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the natural silk filament.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 35, 'question': 'Which Indian organization promotes and supports rural sericulture, khadi spinning, and village silk cottage industries?', 'options': ['Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC)', 'Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)', 'Geological Survey of India (GSI)', 'Automobile Association of Upper India (AAUI)'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'KVIC (Khadi and Village Industries Commission) is the premier Indian statutory body fostering rural cottage industries, hand-spun khadi, and traditional sericulture weaving.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Institutional Heritage:</strong> The Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) was established to empower rural artisans and farmers through self-reliant cottage industries.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Sericulture Link:</strong> Mulberry cultivation, silkworm rearing, and handloom silk weaving are heavily supported by KVIC schemes across India.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the correct national institution.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 36, 'question': 'What is the biological term for the dramatic anatomical transformation from an aquatic tadpole into a leaping terrestrial adult frog?', 'options': ['Metamorphosis', 'Imbibition', 'Sedimentation', 'Transpiration'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Metamorphosis is the biological process by which an animal physically develops after birth or hatching, involving a conspicuous and relatively abrupt change in body structure.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Etymology & Concept:</strong> Derived from Greek (<em>meta</em> = change, <em>morphe</em> = form). It describes major morphological restructuring between developmental stages.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Amphibian Example:</strong> The limbless, gill-breathing, vegetarian tadpole transforms into a four-legged, lung-breathing, carnivorous adult frog.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option A is the correct biological term.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 37, 'question': 'What happens to the tail of an aquatic tadpole during its final stages of transformation into an adult frog?', 'options': ['It drops off into the water like a fallen leaf and rots away', 'It is enzymatically broken down and its nutrients are reabsorbed by the body to nourish growing limbs and lungs', "It transforms into a fifth jumping leg on the frog's belly", 'It grows longer and becomes a poisonous defense stinger'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Through programmed cell death (apoptosis) and lysosomal enzymes, tail tissues are systematically broken down and reabsorbed into the froglet's body to fuel organ restructuring.", 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Apoptosis & Autophagy:</strong> During metamorphic climax, thyroid hormones trigger programmed cell destruction of the tail muscles, vertebrae, and skin.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Nutrient Recycling:</strong> The amino acids and lipids from the degraded tail are transported through the bloodstream to build limbs, eyes, and lungs, leaving no external tail behind.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B provides the accurate biological account.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 38, 'question': 'How does an adult frog breathe when it is resting quietly underwater at the bottom of a pond?', 'options': ['It uses external feathered fish gills on its cheeks', 'It conducts cutaneous gas exchange directly through its thin, moist, blood-vessel-rich skin', 'It extends a long snorkel pipe out of the water surface', 'It stops needing oxygen entirely and enters non-living stasis'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Adult frogs exhibit cutaneous respiration. When submerged, their lungs are inactive and gas exchange occurs entirely across their thin, mucus-covered, vascularized skin.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Cutaneous Respiration:</strong> Amphibian skin is permeable to water and gases. Mucus glands keep the skin moist, allowing dissolved oxygen in pond water to diffuse into subcutaneous capillaries.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Dual Respiratory Modes:</strong> On land, frogs use pulmonary (lung) respiration and buccopharyngeal breathing; underwater, cutaneous respiration takes over.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B is the correct physiological adaptation.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 39, 'question': 'Which of the following comparisons between a tadpole and an adult frog is scientifically ACCURATE?', 'options': ['Tadpole has legs and breathes with lungs; adult frog has a tail and breathes with gills', 'Tadpole is aquatic, has a tail, and breathes with gills; adult frog has four limbs, lacks a tail, and breathes via lungs and moist skin', 'Tadpole lives on trees while adult frog lives inside dry desert cacti', 'Both tadpole and adult frog have feathers and lay hard-shelled bird eggs'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The tadpole is aquatic with a tail and gills; the adult frog is semi-terrestrial, tailless, four-limbed, and breathes using lungs on land and moist skin in water.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Tadpole Traits:</strong> Hydrodynamic tail for propulsion, horny beak for scraping algae, internal/external gills for aquatic respiration, lack of limbs.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Adult Frog Traits:</strong> Long muscular hind legs for leaping, wide mouth with projectile tongue for catching insects, lungs and moist vascular skin for dual respiration, tailless body.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B is completely accurate.</p>\n        "}, {'id': 40, 'question': 'Why is eliminating stagnant water in discarded tyres, coconut shells, and air coolers an effective community health measure?', 'options': ['It makes city streets look aesthetically cleaner for tourists', 'It destroys the essential aquatic breeding habitat of mosquito eggs, wrigglers, and tumblers, preventing the spread of dengue and malaria', "It prevents frogs from jumping into people's living rooms", 'It forces silkworms to weave cocoons on telegraph poles'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Female mosquitoes can only lay eggs in standing water where wrigglers and tumblers develop. Emptying stagnant water eliminates vector breeding sites and curbs disease transmission.', 'solution_html': "\n            <h4 class='font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-1'>Detailed Solution:</h4>\n            <p>1. <strong>Vector Life Cycle Dependence:</strong> Mosquitoes (such as <em>Aedes</em> transmitting dengue and <em>Anopheles</em> transmitting malaria) require stagnant freshwater for their egg, larval, and pupal stages.</p>\n            <p>2. <strong>Source Reduction:</strong> Eliminating small pools of standing water denies adult females egg-laying sites, breaking the reproductive cycle and dramatically slashing vector populations.</p>\n            <p>3. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Option B is the vital public health rationale.</p>\n        "}]
